  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Weakening front and and first of a few waves of \nlow pressure system have lifted NEwards leading to increased\nmoisture and shower activity across ENC. The dissipating front\nand the resulting SFC troughing present once the front has fully\nwashed out will provide a better focal point for greater \ncoverage of showers and storms ahead of a second front which \nwill cross the area this weekend. Out of all the next 3 or 4\ndays days, Thurs looks to have the least amount of activity \nwith showers and thunderstorms being more isolated to widely \nscattered in nature, though this will still be an increase in \nprecip chances as compared to previous days with PoPs generally \naround 30-50% through much of the day Thurs. Activity ongoing \nalong the OBX and SEern zones this morning and then push inland\nas the day goes on. Biggest question marks for today will be \nhow focused activity will be, and how much instability can \nbuild through the day. PWATs do creep back north of 1.75\" and \nlikely get close to 2\" as we approach the evening hours Thurs, \nbut cloud cover may cap CAPE values. Dynamics aloft aren't\nimpressive so showers and tstorms that do develop are not \nanticipated to be very organized. The remnants of the front \nremain around the area tonight into Fri, promoting even greater\nchances for more widespread shower and thunderstorm activity as\nmoisture and instability continues to advect into the region. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...With enough instability in place, and subtly \nincreasing amounts of shear as trough aloft over Canada digs \ntoward the Great Lakes tomorrow, a few isolated stronger storms\naren't completely out of the question. In addition to this, any\nshower or thunderstorm that develops will be an efficient \nrainmaker, potentially bringing torrential rainfall and a risk \nfor flash flooding, mainly in urban and poor drainage areas, and\nadding to the wind risk via waterloading/downbursts. As such, \nSPC continues the Marginal Risk (lvl 1/5) of severe tstorms and\nWPC continues the Slight Risk (lvl 2/4) of excessive rainfall \nfor tomorrow while the FA. Rain continues into the weekend when\na front pushed by the digging trough from the Great Lakes will \ncross the FA first half of the weekend. Temps through the \nweekend remain seasonable with highs in the 80s and lows in the \nupper 60s to mid 70s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Behind the departing cold front, upper level and \nsurface high pressure builds back over the area Sun into early next \nweek. This will bring a return to more climatological precip chances \nalong the seabreeze as well as rising temperatures, low to mid \n90s/mid 70s splits and MaxAppTs in excess of 100deg from TUES onward \nnext week.",
